.TH FKSTRREAD 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkstrread \- read from a file descriptor into a new fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkstrread(int " fd ", size_t " count );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkstrread ()
reads up to
.I count
bytes from the file descriptor
.I fd
with a single call to
.BR read (2),
and returns what was read as a new
.BR fkstring .
Embedded NUL bytes are preserved.
.PP
A buffer of
.IR count "\~+\~1"
bytes is allocated before reading, whatever the amount actually read.
.SH RETURN VALUE
A new
.BR fkstring .
It is empty at end of file, or when
.I count
is 0.
On error,
.BR fkstrread ()
returns NULL, with
.I errno
set by
.BR read (2).
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR read (2).
.BR fkstrread ()
does not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
Like
.BR read (2),
.BR fkstrread ()
may return fewer bytes than requested even before end of file, and does
not retry when interrupted by a signal.
To read to end of file, use
.BR fkslurp (3);
to read a line, use
.BR fkreadline (3).
.PP
Because the full buffer is allocated up front, a large
.I count
costs memory even when little data is available.
The buffer is not shrunk to fit afterwards; use
.BR fkfit (3)
if that matters.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s;
\&
while ((s = fkstrread(fd, 4096)) != NULL && fkstrlen(s) > 0) {
    process(s);
    fkstrdestroy(s);
}
fkstrdestroy(s);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR read (2),
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrwrite (3),
.BR fkslurp (3),
.BR fkreadline (3)
